LESSON 3: FORM AND preventDefault() METHOD
================================================================================
PART 1: OVERVIEW OF THE LESSON
================================================================================

WHAT YOU'LL LEARN IN THIS LESSON
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This lesson focuses on understanding and using preventDefault() when handling
forms in React.

You will understand:

• What default browser form submission means

• Why preventDefault() is commonly used in React forms

• How preventDefault() works

• Preventing native form navigation/reload behavior

• Handling form submission in React

• Creating onSubmit event handlers

• Processing controlled-form data

• Validating data before application-level submission

• Displaying success/error feedback

• Understanding preventDefault() vs stopPropagation()

• Common preventDefault() use cases


WHY THIS LESSON IS IMPORTANT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Understanding preventDefault() is useful because:

• React applications often handle form submission with JavaScript

• Native form navigation may not match the application's desired behavior

• Form state can be validated before application logic continues

• API requests can be started from the submit handler

• Error messages can be displayed without navigating away

• Success states can be displayed inside the current application

• It is an important browser event API concept

• It helps distinguish browser behavior from React state behavior


================================================================================
PART 2: UNDERSTANDING DEFAULT FORM BEHAVIOR
================================================================================

WHAT IS DEFAULT FORM BEHAVIOR?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

HTML forms have built-in browser submission behavior.

Example:

<form
  action="/users"
  method="post"
>

  <input
    name="name"
  />

  <button type="submit">
    Submit
  </button>

</form>


When submitted, the browser can perform the form's native submission action
according to the form configuration.


COMMON NATIVE FORM SUBMISSION FLOW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

User Submits Form
        ↓
Browser Creates Submission
        ↓
Uses Form Configuration
        ↓
action + method
        ↓
Browser Sends Request
        ↓
Browser May Navigate to Resulting Document


IMPORTANT:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

It is more precise to say:

preventDefault() prevents the browser's DEFAULT SUBMIT ACTION.

Whether a browser visibly "reloads the same page" or navigates elsewhere
depends on the form and resulting navigation.


================================================================================
WHY DOES DEFAULT FORM BEHAVIOR EXIST?
================================================================================

HTML forms existed long before React.

Traditional pattern:

Browser
   ↓
HTML Form
   ↓
Server Request
   ↓
Server Processes Form
   ↓
Server Returns Document
   ↓
Browser Displays Document


This behavior is still useful.

Native form submission is NOT inherently wrong.

It may be appropriate for:

• Traditional server-rendered applications

• Progressive enhancement

• Applications intentionally using browser-based form navigation

• Workflows where native HTML submission is desired


================================================================================
WHY INTERCEPT SUBMISSION IN A REACT APP?
================================================================================

A React application may instead want:

Form
 ↓
React Handler
 ↓
Validate
 ↓
API Request
 ↓
Update React State
 ↓
Display Feedback


without handing navigation control to the browser's default form action.


================================================================================
NATIVE SUBMISSION VS CUSTOM REACT HANDLING
================================================================================

+-----------------------+--------------------------+----------------------------+
| AREA                  | NATIVE SUBMISSION        | CUSTOM REACT HANDLING      |
+-----------------------+--------------------------+----------------------------+
| Browser default       | Allowed                  | Prevented                  |
| Navigation            | May occur                | Controlled by application  |
| React validation      | Not required             | Can run before API logic   |
| React state workflow  | May be interrupted       | Can continue in same UI    |
| API handling          | Browser/native form      | JavaScript can perform it  |
| Feedback              | New document or browser  | React can render feedback  |
| Use case              | Native/server workflow   | Client-driven workflow     |
+-----------------------+--------------------------+----------------------------+


================================================================================
PART 3: UNDERSTANDING preventDefault()
================================================================================

WHAT IS preventDefault()?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

preventDefault() is a method on an event object.

Example:

const handleSubmit = (event) => {

  event.preventDefault()

}


It tells the browser:

"Do not perform the default action associated with this event."


================================================================================
FORM EXAMPLE
================================================================================

const handleSubmit = (event) => {

  event.preventDefault()


  console.log(
    'Handle submission in application code'
  )
}


return (
  <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>

    {/* Fields */}

    <button type="submit">
      Submit
    </button>

  </form>
)


FLOW:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

User Submits Form
       ↓
submit Event
       ↓
handleSubmit(event)
       ↓
event.preventDefault()
       ↓
Native Submission Prevented
       ↓
Application Code Continues


================================================================================
WHAT preventDefault() DOES
================================================================================

• Prevents the event's cancelable default action

• Allows the current event handler to continue executing

• Lets the application choose what happens next

• Is commonly used when React handles a form submission itself


================================================================================
WHAT preventDefault() DOES NOT DO
================================================================================

preventDefault() does NOT automatically:

• Validate your form

• Send data to an API

• Reset the fields

• Display success messages

• Stop event propagation

• Update React state

• Make every event cancelable


Those behaviors require separate application logic.


================================================================================
preventDefault() PATTERN
================================================================================

const handleSubmit = (event) => {

  // Stop browser's native submit behavior

  event.preventDefault()


  // Your logic starts here

  console.log(
    'Form Data:',
    formData
  )
}


