================================================================================
WHY noValidate?
================================================================================

The example uses:

<form
  noValidate
  ...
>


because the lesson is demonstrating custom application validation.

Without noValidate, the browser's built-in constraint validation may stop
submission before your submit handler runs in some invalid-input scenarios.

IMPORTANT:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This does NOT mean native browser validation is bad.

Choose deliberately between:

• Native constraint validation

• Custom application validation

• A combination appropriate to the product


================================================================================
RESET: IMPORTANT CORRECTION
================================================================================

A form reset is different from form submission.

With controlled React inputs, you generally want reset to update React state:

const handleReset = () => {

  setFormData(
    INITIAL_FORM
  )

  setErrors({})

}


Then:

<form onReset={handleReset}>


and:

<button type="reset">
  Clear Form
</button>


You do NOT normally need:

event.preventDefault()

simply to use a controlled React reset.


Why?

Because the application state is the source of truth and the reset handler
can restore that state.


