PART 6: COMMON preventDefault() USE CASES
================================================================================

1. FORM SUBMISSION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const handleSubmit = (
  event
) => {

  event.preventDefault()

  // Custom processing

}


================================================================================
2. LINK CLICK
================================================================================

const handleLinkClick = (
  event
) => {

  event.preventDefault()

  // Perform custom behavior instead
  // of following the link.
}


Use this only when preventing navigation is actually required.

If something behaves like a button rather than navigation, a <button>
element may be more semantically appropriate than an <a> element.


================================================================================
3. CONTEXT MENU
================================================================================

const handleContextMenu = (
  event
) => {

  event.preventDefault()

  // Optional custom context-menu behavior

}


================================================================================
4. DRAG AND DROP
================================================================================

In browser drag/drop implementations, preventing the default behavior on
appropriate drag events is commonly part of enabling a custom drop target.

Example:

const handleDragOver = (
  event
) => {

  event.preventDefault()

}


================================================================================
5. KEYBOARD BEHAVIOR
================================================================================

const handleKeyDown = (
  event
) => {

  if (
    event.key === 'Enter'
  ) {

    event.preventDefault()

    // Custom behavior
  }
}


IMPORTANT:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Do not prevent keyboard defaults unnecessarily.

Native keyboard behavior often exists for usability/accessibility reasons.


================================================================================
6. BUTTON DEFAULTS
================================================================================

Inside a form:

<button>
  Click
</button>


defaults to a submit button.

If the button is NOT intended to submit:

<button type="button">
  Click
</button>


This is usually clearer than relying on:

event.preventDefault()

inside every non-submit button handler.


================================================================================
USE CASE TABLE
================================================================================

+----------------------+-------------------------+----------------------------+
| EVENT                | DEFAULT ACTION          | POSSIBLE CUSTOM HANDLING   |
+----------------------+-------------------------+----------------------------+
| Form submit          | Native form submit      | React/API submission       |
| Link click           | Navigate                | Custom routing/behavior    |
| Context menu         | Browser context menu    | Custom context menu        |
| Drag over/drop       | Browser drag behavior   | Custom drop workflow       |
| Keyboard             | Key-specific default    | App shortcut where needed  |
| Submit button        | Submit enclosing form   | Use type="button" if not   |
+----------------------+-------------------------+----------------------------+


================================================================================
PART 7: preventDefault() VS stopPropagation()
================================================================================

THEY SOLVE DIFFERENT PROBLEMS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

preventDefault():

Stops the browser's default action.


stopPropagation():

Stops the event from propagating further through the event path.


================================================================================
COMPARISON TABLE
================================================================================

+----------------------+----------------------------+---------------------------+
| AREA                 | preventDefault()           | stopPropagation()         |
+----------------------+----------------------------+---------------------------+
| Purpose              | Cancel default action      | Stop propagation          |
| Form example         | Prevent native submit      | Stop event reaching parent|
| Link example         | Prevent navigation         | Stop click propagation    |
| Parent handlers      | Doesn't itself stop them   | Can stop propagation      |
| Default browser action| Can cancel it             | Doesn't itself cancel it  |
| Can use together     | Yes                        | Yes                       |
+----------------------+----------------------------+---------------------------+


================================================================================
EXAMPLE: preventDefault()
================================================================================

<a
  href="/products"
  onClick={(event) => {

    event.preventDefault()

    console.log(
      'Navigation prevented'
    )

  }}
>
  Products
</a>


Click event can still propagate.

Only the default navigation is prevented.


================================================================================
EXAMPLE: stopPropagation()
================================================================================

<div
  onClick={() => {

    console.log(
      'Parent clicked'
    )

  }}
>

  <button
    type="button"
    onClick={(event) => {

      event.stopPropagation()

      console.log(
        'Button clicked'
      )

    }}
  >

    Click

  </button>

</div>


Here:

Button handler runs.

Parent click handler is not reached through normal propagation from that event.


================================================================================
USING BOTH
================================================================================

const handleClick = (
  event
) => {

  event.preventDefault()

  event.stopPropagation()


  // Custom logic
}


Use both only when the interaction genuinely requires:

1. Default action cancellation

AND

2. Propagation prevention


